  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/... \nAs we transition into a drier weather pattern mid week, we will\nstill have to deal with periods of breezy to windy conditions. \nThe upper low is forecast to move east northeastward from northern\nArizona into Colorado on Wednesday leaving a tight pressure \ngradient across much of our area. This should bring increasing \nnorthwesterly winds across southeast California and far western \nArizona early Wednesday likely peaking late Wednesday morning \nbefore the gradient relaxes. Wind gusts of 25-30 mph can be \nexpected across much of the Lower CO River Valley Wednesday \nmorning into early afternoon. \n\nTemperatures Wednesday will remain well below normal across the\narea with a somewhat chilly start to the morning with lows in the\n50s across the lower deserts to easily into the 40s for higher \nterrain locations. Highs Wednesday should be very similar to\nTuesday, although some warming across the western lower deserts\nmay push highs back into the lower 80s. \n\nModels are finally coming into better agreement for late this\nweek into the weekend showing shortwave energy and an upper level\njet max diving southwestward through the region on Thursday. At\nthe same time a strong upper level ridge just off the West Coast\nwill move inland. For now, it seems this set up will result in a \nweak cut-off low forming somewhere to our southwest by Friday with\nridging settling in just to our north. To complicate things even \nmore, a polar air mass will quickly dive through the Plains States\nThursday potentially bringing a back-door cool front into eastern\nArizona Friday morning. Even though this is a complex pattern, it\nreally won't amount to much for our area as dry air will remain \nentrenched enough to keep rain chances below 10%. \n\nHowever, it is looking increasingly likely a strong pressure \ngradient will develop across Arizona by Friday leading to breezy \nto locally windy easterly winds developing Thursday night into \nFriday morning. NBM guidance does show the potential for Advisory\nlevel winds, but from past experience these winds tend to be \noverdone. We should at least expect occasional wind gusts of 25-35\nmph to maybe some 35-40 mph possible across far southern Gila \nCounty, Pinal County, into southern Maricopa County. These gusty \neasterly winds will be more pronounced during the latter half of \nthe morning before diminishing some during the afternoon. Guidance\nsuggests a repeat of these gusty winds on Saturday before the \ngradient begins to relax into Sunday. Moderating temperatures are\nforecast for late week with highs warming into the 80s by \nThursday and likely staying at or just below normal through next \nweekend.",
